/*
:name: latch_sim_nonref_clock_window
:type: simulation
*/

// SILENT SIM MISCOMPILE, minimal mirror of minion's `prim_rf_1r1w_diff_preview`
// (the frontend instruction buffer `u_inst_data`).
//
// THE SHAPE. A module declares TWO clock input ports. Its transparent-LOW latch
// is gated on ONE of them (`preview_clk`) while its state sits on the OTHER
// (`rf_clk`), and the parent ties both to the same net. That is minion verbatim:
//     always_latch  if (!preview_clk_i) wr_data_en_1p_q = wr_data_en_1p_next_i;
//     always_ff @(negedge rf_clk_i) if (wr_data_en_1p_q[j]) mem[..] <= ..
//
// THE BUG. cgen_sim models latch transparency by emitting the flop's next-state
// TWICE — a "low window" with the reference clock forced to 0 and a "high
// window" with it forced to 1 — and keeping the low result as the hold value.
// But it forces only the pin `clock_input_of()` chose. Here that is `rf_clk`,
// so `preview_clk` is never substituted and BOTH windows evaluate it at its live
// level: they come out as literally the same expression, the hold leg is
// unreachable, and the latch is transparent for the WHOLE period instead of one
// phase. Emitted C++ from minion, verbatim:
//     cg_59 = (__in.preview_clk_i == 0);   // "low" window
//     cg_61 = (__in.preview_clk_i == 0);   // "high" window -- IDENTICAL
//
// WHAT IT COST. The enable rose a full period early, so the instruction buffer
// was written before its fetch line arrived; minion decoded a stale word, took a
// spurious illegal-instruction trap at pc=0x4 and replayed pc=0 forever -- 1,428
// retired instructions in 20k cycles where Verilator retires 12,240 on the same
// RTL. `lhd sim` reported 0 errors and 0 warnings.
//
// STATUS: THIS FILE CURRENTLY PASSES. It reproduces the defective EMISSION, not
// yet a wrong value — the asserts below are satisfied because `clk` is the tick
// and is never driven, so `preview_clk` folds to a constant and the collapsed
// hold leg does not change what this particular stimulus observes. Minion needs
// the gated `clock_gated`/`clk_i` pair for the collapse to become an observable
// one-period-early write. Treat this as the SMALL REPRODUCER of the emission
// bug (checked by grep, below) and tighten the asserts once the phase the
// consumer should see is settled against an oracle; do not read a green run
// here as evidence the bug is gone.
//
// HOW TO SEE IT WITHOUT AN ORACLE. Grep the generated child for its two window
// guards; today they are the same expression:
//     lhd sim inou/prp/tests/sim/latch_sim_nonref_clock_window.prp \
//         --setup-only --workdir /tmp/w
//     grep -n '_low\|_din' /tmp/w/sim/prevrf*.cpp
// A correct emission gates the low window on a forced 0 and the high window on a
// forced 1, so the hold leg is reachable and `en_q` survives one phase.
//
// NOT the sole-clock case: flop_sim_negedge_sole_clock.prp pins that one (a
// design with NO register on the implicit reference clock gets no edge detection
// at all). Here the parent supplies a proper reference clock and the child is an
// ordinary instance -- only the latch's gating port is the odd one out.
mod prevrf(preview_clk:bool, rf_clk:bool, wr_en_next:bool, d:u8) -> (q:u8@[0]) {
  reg en_q:bool:[latch=true]                        // transparent while preview_clk is LOW
  reg mem:u8:[clock_pin=ref rf_clk, posclk=false] = 0
  q = mem
  if !preview_clk {
    en_q = wr_en_next
  }
  if en_q {
    mem = d
  }
}

// The parent ties BOTH child clock ports to its own clock, exactly as
// minion_frontend_thread_buffer does (`preview_clk_i(clock_gated)`,
// `rf_clk_i(clk_i)`, one net at every instantiation site). `clk` is the tick --
// the testbench never drives it, just as minion's never drives clk_i.
mod pvtop(clk:bool, wr_en_next:bool, d:u8) -> (q:u8@[0]) {
  q = prevrf(preview_clk=clk, rf_clk=clk, wr_en_next=wr_en_next, d=d)
}

test pvtop.enable_survives_one_phase {
  mut acc = pvtop
  // wr_en_next is high for EXACTLY one tick (5). A latch that holds for a phase
  // admits exactly one write; a latch that is transparent all period admits the
  // write a period early, so the captured datum is d of the wrong tick.
  tick 10 {
    acc.wr_en_next = clock == 5
    acc.d          = 100 + clock
    step
    // Nothing may be written before the enable is even asserted.
    if clock <= 4 {
      assert(acc.q == 0, "no write before wr_en_next is asserted")
    }
    // Exactly one datum is ever captured, and it is the one presented while the
    // enable was high -- never an earlier tick's.
    if clock >= 6 {
      assert(acc.q == 105, "the single enabled write captured d of the enabled tick, and holds")
    }
  }
}
